  "query_preview": "/SUNDAY THROUGH WEDNESDAY/...    \nBy Sunday, anticyclonic flow aloft will transition to quasi-zonal as \nthe upper low originally off the British Columbia Coast digs \nsoutheastward into the Central CONUS and remnant energy from the \ntrough originally near Hawaii quickly ejects eastward through the \nSouthwest US. Despite decreasing midlevel heights, lower level \nthermal profiles are not expected to respond strongly, and so lower \ndeserts highs should continue to maintain at least the middle 70s \nthrough early next week. \n\nEnsembles are starting to come into better agreement with respect to \na more significant Pacific low developing offshore the CA Coast\nearly next week and then tracking a cold front through the region\nduring the Tuesday-Wednesday timeframe. There are still \nconsiderable discrepancies in timing and the exact trajectory of \nthis low, however, a few things are becoming more certain. H5 \nheights aloft at the center of this Pacific low will deepen \noffshore early next week, with NAEFS mean values drop below the \n1st percentile of climatology. The Pacific low will displace \ndownstream shortwave ridging further east as it nears the region, \nresulting in considerable height packing overhead. This setup will\nsupport a strong, cyclonically curved jet streak which will \nattend the low as it progresses through the region. Strong \nregional pressure and temperature gradients ahead of the low \n(especially ahead of the cold front) will lead to a period of \nwidespread gusty winds with speeds likely peaking in the afternoon\nTuesday as mixing taps the higher momentum air aloft. Moisture \nwill also increase, although the extent of moisture return will \ndepend on how far south the Pacific low digs. Strong southwesterly\nflow aloft will likely result in IVT values surging in excess of \nthe 90th percentile of climatology, around 250-350 kg/(ms). \nHowever, this will be relatively short lived due to the fast- \nmoving, progressive nature of this system, and the best moisture \nwill be confined ahead of the front. A brief period with moist \nisentropic ascent, modest WAA, and frontogenetic forcing could \nresult in at least one band of showers with possibly some embedded\nT-storms. Model uncertainty at this lead time is still too great \nto pin down any of these details. It is still conceivable this \nsystem passes mostly north of the forecast area, and the southern \nextent of a mostly dry cold front brushes the lower deserts during\nthe middle of next week. Regardless, as is typical with systems\nlike this, the Central AZ high terrain to the north and east of\nPhoenix is favored to receive the better rainfall totals. Rain\nshadowing effects over Southeast CA and far Southwest AZ will also\nlikely play a significant role in limiting rainfall totals.",
